The moment a cut completes is the moment `ready` used to go quiet.

Every member of `M-s0` is done, so there is no work to pick up: a milestone is a
checkpoint and `ready` offers work (ADR-0008). But every task in the next cut is
waiting on that milestone, and someone has to close it. Printing nothing at all
was indistinguishable from a finished plan or a broken config, and the fourth
pilot met exactly this on its first cut boundary (`A1-105`).

```
$ aplan ready
nothing is ready. `M-s0`'s members are all done: `aplan close M-s0` opens what follows it.

```

`why` agrees with `ready` about what a milestone is. It used to answer "M-s0 is
ready: nothing blocks it", which is the word `ready` uses for something it will
not offer.

```
$ aplan why M-s0
M-s0 is a cut and its members are all done: `aplan close M-s0` opens what follows it

```

A cut that scopes no work is not a finished one. `all()` over an empty list is
true, so `M-empty` read as "members all done" and the reader was told to record
the completion of a cut with nothing in it. That is `APLAN021`'s finding, and
`ready` leaves it alone.

```
$ aplan why M-empty
M-empty is a cut: nothing blocks it

```

A task that is over is not "ready" either, which is the same word used for the
same wrong reason: `ready` offers neither a checkpoint nor a finished task.

```
$ aplan why A-001
A-001 is `done`: there is nothing to do

```

And the task in the next cut still gets the plain answer, because a milestone
blocking it is an ordinary blocker.

```
$ aplan why B-001
B-001 is blocked by:
  M-s0 [todo]  Foundations

```
